###### Abstract

We present new X-ray timing and spectral results on the 8.0-second X-ray pulsar CXOU J010043.1-721134 from a series of observations using the _Chandra X-ray Observatory_. We find a spin period in 2004 January of 8.020392\\pm 0.000009 seconds. Comparison of this to 2001 _Chandra_ observations implies a period derivative \\dot{P}=(1.88\\pm 0.08)\\times 10^{-11} s s{}^{-1}, leading to an inferred dipole surface magnetic field of 3.9\\times 10^{14} G. The spectrum is well fit to an absorbed blackbody of temperature kT=0.38\\pm 0.02 keV with a power law tail of photon index \\Gamma=2.0\\pm 0.6. We find that the source has an unabsorbed X-ray flux (0.5-10 keV) of 4^{+2}_{-1}\\times 10^{-13} erg cm{}^{-2} s{}^{-1} and a corresponding X-ray luminosity of \\sim 2\\times 10^{35} erg s{}^{-1} for a distance of 60 kpc. These properties support classification of CXOU J010043.1-721134 as the seventh confirmed anomalous X-ray pulsar, the eleventh confirmed magnetar, and the first magnetar to be identified in the Small Magellanic Cloud.

